	</item>
	<item>
		<title>Care and keeping</title>
		<link>/care/</link>
		<pubDate>2026-08-30 12:00:00</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">/?p=102</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="prose__lead">None of this is precious, but a few habits will keep everything looking new for years rather than months.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">The three rules</h2>
  <p>Keep it out of the shower, the pool and the sea. Chlorine and salt are what actually kill gold plating.</p>
  <p>Put jewelry on after perfume, lotion and hairspray, never before. Alcohol dulls enamel faster than anything else.</p>
  <p>Store pieces flat and separately. Cords twist and chains tangle when things get thrown in together.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">Cleaning</h2>
  <p>A dry soft cloth is enough for almost everything. For enamel and glass, breathe on it and buff, the way you would with glasses.</p>
  <p>Never use jewelry dip or silver polish on plated brass. It strips the plating and it does not come back.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">Pearls specifically</h2>
  <p>Freshwater pearls are the softest thing we use. Wipe them after wearing, keep them away from anything acidic, and do not store them anywhere hot.</p>]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>102</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-08-30 12:00:00]]></wp:post_date>
