	<item>
		<title>Glass, not plastic, and why it matters</title>
		<link>/glass-not-plastic/</link>
		<pubDate>2026-08-30 12:00:00</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">/?p=107</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="prose__lead">The fruit could be resin. It would be cheaper, lighter and easier. Here is what you would lose.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">Weight is information</h2>
  <p>Glass has heft. When you put on a glass cherry necklace you feel it settle, and that weight is most of what makes a piece feel real rather than novelty. Resin fruit sits on you like a sticker.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">Colour that does not go</h2>
  <p>Glass colour is in the material. Resin and painted plastic sit on the surface, and surfaces rub, especially anything that lives against skin or in a bag. Two summers in, painted fruit looks tired. Glass looks the same as the day it was made.</p>
  <p>The catch is that glass breaks if you are careless with it. We would still rather sell you something that can break than something that slowly goes ugly.</p>]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>107</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-08-30 12:00:00]]></wp:post_date>
		<wp:post_date_gmt><![CDATA[2026-08-30 12:00:00]]></wp:post_date_gmt>
		<wp:comment_status><![CDATA[closed]]></wp:comment_status>
		<wp:ping_status><![CDATA[closed]]></wp:ping_status>
		<wp:post_name><![CDATA[glass-not-plastic]]></wp:post_name>
		<wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
		<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
